Done: 1.6.3. `noctalia-core::process::detached` (crates/noctalia-core/src/
  process/detached.rs): a genuinely different execution strategy from 1.6.1/
  1.6.2, which both build on `std::process::Command` — this one needs a real
  double-fork + `setsid` (so the exec'd process reparents away from us
  entirely, matching launcher app-activation semantics), which `Command`
  can't do, so it's built directly on raw `libc::fork`/`setsid`/`execvp`.
  Ported `doubleForkExecDetached` (report-pipe pid handoff, three-way fork
  branch, intermediate `setsid`+second-fork+immediate-exit, grandchild
  chdir/setenv(XDG_ACTIVATION_TOKEN,DESKTOP_STARTUP_ID)/attachStdioToDevNull/
  execvp-or-_exit(127)), `writePipeOrIgnore`, `attachStdioToDevNull`, plus the
  callback-less spawn family built on it: `launch_detached` (port of
  `runAsync(args, activationToken, workingDir)`), `launch_detached_shell`
  (port of plain `runAsync(command)`, /bin/sh -lc composition, no callbacks),
  `launch_detached_tracked`/`terminate_tracked`, `launch_first_available`,
  and PATH-search `command_exists`/`resolve_privilege_escalator`. Named
  differently from 1.6.2's `run_async`/`run_async_shell` (which are the
  *callback-carrying* worker-thread family) since both share the C++'s
  `runAsync` name via overloading but are different engines under the hood —
  documented in this module's doc comment.
  `WIFEXITED`/`WEXITSTATUS` reimplemented via `std::process::ExitStatus::
  from_raw(status).success()` rather than hand-rolling the glibc bit macros —
  verified equivalent (exit-0 true, nonzero-exit false, signal-killed false)
  with a small standalone test program, not just assumed.
  Two hazards recorded in the module doc comment as inherited-from-C++, not
  fixed here (matches "match C++ behavior over improving it"): forking a
  multithreaded process is inherently fragile (only the calling thread
  survives; a lock held by another thread at fork time can deadlock the
  child before it execs) — the C++ has the same exposure; and neither
  implementation takes any lock before `fork()`, which matters concretely for
  this crate's own test binary specifically (see below).
  Discovered and fixed a real cross-test hazard this task introduces: 1.6.1's
  `env_overrides_apply_to_child` test relies on `std::process::Command::
  spawn`'s internal `ENV_LOCK` (a std-internal RwLock) being synchronized
  against `std::env::set_var`/`remove_var`, which is true for `Command::
  spawn` but NOT for this module's raw `libc::fork()` — a `set_var` racing
  this module's `fork()` on another thread could hand the child a torn
  `environ`. Added `process::test_support::ENV_MUTATION_LOCK` (a shared
  `Mutex<()>`) and audited every test in `detached.rs`/`core_exec.rs`/
  `async_exec.rs` for whether it mutates env and/or reaches a real fork
  (some `launch_detached_tracked`/`launch_first_available` calls return
  early before ever forking and correctly need no lock); every test that
  does either now holds the lock for its full risky window, including
  retrofitting core_exec.rs's already-committed 1.6.1 test (justified: this
  new task provably reopened a gap in already-committed code, not scope
  creep).
  Also discovered this NixOS host ships a genuine `run0` (systemd 256+),
  which would make a naive "prepend a fake PATH dir" test for
  `resolve_privilege_escalator`'s run0-vs-pkexec priority spuriously pass or
  fail depending on what's really installed; fixed by fully replacing PATH
  in those two tests rather than prepending, making them host-independent.
  Tests: ported `detachedAsyncInheritsLaunchEnvironment` (env vars containing
  a space, matching the C++'s exact path string) and
  `commandExistsRejectsDirectories` verbatim; added new coverage with no C++
