  this ports both use the C set.
  Pre-done review (rule 3, fresh subagent): first pass found one real bug,
  not just nitpicks — `parse_leading_f32` (the hand-rolled float-prefix
  scanner behind `try_parse_css_color`'s channel/alpha/hue/percent
  parsing) accepted a leading `+` on the mantissa (e.g. `"rgb(+128,0,0)"`),
  which the reviewer confirmed by compiling a probe against this host's
  actual `std::from_chars` diverges from: C++'s float grammar rejects a
  leading `+` outside the exponent, so the whole component (and thus the
  whole color) fails to parse in the C++ where the pre-fix Rust wrongly
  succeeded. Fixed: only `-` is accepted as a mantissa sign now; the
  exponent's own `+`/`-` (e.g. `"1e+3"`) is unaffected, matching C++
  exactly per the same probe. Added regression tests for both the
  now-rejected `+` forms and the still-accepted exponent form. The
  reviewer's second, lower-severity finding — `std::from_chars` parses
  `"nan"`/`"inf"`/`"infinity"` as valid floats and C++'s range checks
  don't reject `NaN` (a NaN comparison is always false), so a channel
  token of `"nan"` silently produces a `NaN` channel in the C++ — was
  deliberately *not* replicated: nothing in this crate's ported call
  sites reaches `try_parse_css_color` yet (`colorSpecFromConfigString`
  only ever calls `try_parse_hex_color`), there's no C++ test locking the
  NaN behavior in, and matching it would mean deliberately choosing to
  let NaN into a `Color` for no functional benefit. Recorded as a
  documented, tested divergence on `parse_leading_f32` itself rather than
  silently differing. Every other reviewed item (bit-shift/mask hex
  parsing, HSV/HSL sextant math and lerp_hsv's shortest-hue-path
